after days of calling for calm in  ukraine and downplaying u.s warnings of  an imminent invasion a change of tone  from president vladimir zelinsky in an  address to the nation  we are being threatened by a big war and  a date of military invasion has been  scheduled once again it is not for the  first time a war is being conducted  systematically against us on all fronts  on the military front the military  contingent has increased around our  borders on the diplomatic front they are  trying to deprive us of the right to  decide foreign policy on our own  it came after a visit by the german  chancellor to ukraine the latest western  leader on a diplomatic mission to  diffuse tensions with russia and avert a  new war in europe  after more than two hours of talks with  zelensky schultz promised keeve more  german financial aid and issued a  warning to moscow  ukraine's sovereignty and territorial  integrity are non-negotiable for germany  we therefore expect russia to take clear  steps to de-escalate the current  tensions  for germany it is clear that further  military aggression against ukraine  would have serious political economic  and geostrategic consequences for russia  solenski said ukraine must join nato  because the country's security was  inseparable from europe  as for our future alliances our  ambitions you know very well that this  is our wish but apart from that there is  a war in the east we believe that nato  membership will ensure our security  silence was forced to clarify ukraine's  position on nato after comments earlier  in the day made by ukraine's ambassador  to london that suggested that ukraine  could be flexible when it comes to its  ambitions to join the military alliance  comments the ambassador later retracted  we are not a member of nato right now  and to avoid war we are ready for many  concessions and that's what we are doing  in our conversations with russians but  it has nothing to do with nato which is  enshrined in constitution  moscow doesn't want ukraine in nato and  says that the west has ignored its  security demands but in a possible sign  of hope russia's foreign minister has  advised president vladimir putin to  continue with dialogue  our possibilities aren't exhausted of  course security talks can't continue  endlessly but at this stage i would  propose to continue and increase them  all right  a move that will be welcomed by schultz  who will meet the kremlin leader on  tuesday in what some say could be a last  chance for diplomacy natasha butler al  jazeera kiev
 

